      "text": "To solve this problem, we can look for a number that, when divided by $4$, has a remainder of $3$ and when divided by $5$ has a remainder of $2$. We start with a small value of $n$ and keep checking until we find the smallest value that satisfies both conditions.\n\nWe have the following congruences:\n\n$n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$\n$n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}$\n\nWe can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem to find the smallest positive value of $n$ that satisfies both conditions.\n\nSince $n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$, we can write $n$ as:\n\n$n = 4a + 3$\n\nNow we substitute this expression of $n$ into its second congruence:\n\n$4a + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$a \\equiv -1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}$\n\nThis means that $a$ must be $5k + 4$ for some integer $k$. Substituting this expression of $a$ into the expression of $n$, we get:\n\n$n = 4(5k + 4) + 3 = 20k + 19$\n\nThe smallest possible value of $n$ that is greater than $10$ is when $k = 1$, giving us:\n\n$n = 20 \\cdot 1 + 19 = 39$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $n$ that satisfies the conditions of the problem is $\\boxed{39}$.",
