      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest value of \\( n \\) greater than 10 that satisfies the following conditions:\n1. \\( n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\)\n2. \\( n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\)\n\nWe start by expressing \\( n \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). Next, we substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying the congruence, we get:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\(-1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\), the congruence becomes:\n\\[ 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nWe can divide both sides by 4, noting that 4 and 5 are coprime, so 4 has a multiplicative inverse modulo 5. The inverse of 4 modulo 5 is 4, because \\( 4 \\cdot 4 = 16 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\). Thus, we have:\n\\[ k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means \\( k = 5m + 1 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting back into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n\\[ n = 4(5m + 1) + 3 = 20m + 4 + 3 = 20m + 7 \\]\nSo, \\( n \\) can be written as:\n\\[ n = 20m + 7 \\]\nWe are asked to find the smallest value of \\( n \\) greater than 10. For \\( m = 0 \\), we get:\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 0 + 7 = 7 \\]\nSince 7 is not greater than 10, we try \\( m = 1 \\):\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 1 + 7 = 27 \\]\nSince 27 is greater than 10, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that meets the criteria is:\n\\[ \\boxed{27} \\]",
