      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the given problem, we need to find the smallest integer \\( n \\) greater than 10 such that when Ray climbs the stairs \\( m \\) steps at a time, he has 3 steps left when climbing \\( 4 \\) steps and \\( 2 \\) steps left when climbing \\( 5 \\) steps.\n\nLet's translate this into a system of congruences:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nn \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\\\\nn \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe start by simplifying the problem. Let's express \\( n \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 \\]\n\nSubstitute \\( n = 4k + 3 \\) into the second congruence:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\\[ 4k + 3 - 2 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nDivide both sides by \\( 4 \\):\n\\[ k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nSo, \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[ k = 5j + 1 \\]\n\nSubstitute \\( k = 5j + 1 \\) back into \\( n = 4k + 3 \\):\n\\[ n = 4(5j + 1) + 3 \\]\n\\[ n = 20j + 4 + 3 \\]\n\\[ n = 20j + 7 \\]\n\nThe smallest value of \\( n \\) greater than 10 is obtained when \\( j = 0 \\):\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 0 + 7 = 7 \\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]",
